WEBVTT

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:07.340
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic from CECS Department at Cal State Long Beach. Now our next topic

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:15.680
is subtraction and please make sure to understand this is subtraction of unsigned numbers. So

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:22.520
let us first look at the rules for subtraction. If we have two binary numbers, A and B, we're

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:28.820
just going to look at them as their single bit. We will have two components of our result.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:37.800
going to be borrowed and the other one is going to be a result itself. So this is going to be

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:49.400
both applied for a result of a minus b operation. If we have 0 minus 0, the result is 0 and we

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:57.520
need not to borrow anything. If we have 0 minus 1, that is the most complicated case.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:06.380
In this case, we have to borrow 1, but also our result is going to be 1.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:15.380
If we have 1 minus 0, the result is going to be 1. No borrow, necessary.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:20.380
And 1 minus 1, 0, no borrow.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.380
Let us look at a couple of examples that go with this.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:38.260
The first example is 3-bit number. I like to leave an extra space below numbers to write my

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:44.020
borrow. For me, that is more intuitive than writing them on top like we did for Carrie.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:02:00.100
1 minus 1 is 0, so there is no borrow. Those two zeros are total 0. 1 minus 0 is 1. Again,

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:08.580
there is no border. These two zeros total, they are zero, zero minus zero, zero, and this is

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:15.780
final, final result. To double check always you can go and convert things to decimal.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:28.480
So example number two. Let's try one zero one minus zero zero one.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:40.570
extra space for borrow, the result goes under the line. When we have 0 minus 1 we

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:51.770
need to have result 1 but we also have borrow that is 1. 0 and plus 1 is total 1

00:02:51.770 --> 00:03:00.770
so 1 minus 1 is 0, borrow 0, those two zeros when they add it up they are total

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:09.410
zero, so zero minus zero is zero. If you think about this, this is decimal two, this is decimal

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:23.620
one, this is decimal one. So far so good. Let's look at other couple of examples. Zero zero zero

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:32.980
minus zero zero one. That one is going to be interesting. Okay, we leave space for borrow.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:43.690
if we have 0 minus 1, our result is 1 and we have to borrow 1. Those two

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:52.050
numbers are total 1. So again, 0 minus 1, result is 1, we have to borrow 1. Those

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:58.490
two are again 1, 0 minus 1, this is 1, we have another one that we are

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:05.920
borrowing here. However, result is only 3 bits. Remember what we were talking

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:20.060
overflow before. So let's look at the final complicated example. We will have

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:43.390
four numbers and we are going to have space for both. 1 minus 1 is 0 and we have

00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:58.650
0 both. 1 plus 0 is 0, sorry, 1 plus 0 is 1. 0 minus 1 is 1 but we have another one

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:10.170
So these two are going to actually make a binary two.

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:24.650
So to better understand this example, I'm going to single out this set of two times two bits.

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:38.340
So we have, we have 1, 1, minus 0, 1. So these two have to be added to each other. So it's

00:05:38.340 --> 00:06:00.680
1, 1, minus 1, 0. 1 minus 0 is 1. And 1 minus 1 is 0. You have to notice that by creating

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:10.480
this kind of artificial one, that's going to be actually similar to, sorry, it's not going to be similar, it's going to be corresponding to the

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:28.880
borrow so 1 minus 1 and 1 is going to be 1 but we will have borrow 1 here 0 plus 1 is 1 so 1 this

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:40.180
one minus this one down is 0. One thing that you can notice is that unsigned numbers when you

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:46.380
subtract them things can get really complicated and especially they can get

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:53.440
complicated because we are not used to binary system and binary borrow that

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:58.100
kind of gets it too complicated. Hope this was helpful.

